@namespace Fhi.Munin.Explorer.Blazor
@using Fhi.Munin.Explorer.Contracts

@* Signed out there is nothing here at all — not an empty frame, not a sign-in prompt this package
   has no business wording. The host knows how its readers sign in; the package does not. *@
@if (State?.IsAuthenticated == true)
{
    @* No class. This root carried `munin-explorer munin-explorer-container`, and both are page
       layout: measured in helsedata, `munin-explorer` computes to
       `display: grid; grid-template-columns: 384px 576px` — the shell's own sidebar-and-results
       grid — and `munin-explorer-container` is `display: flex; flex-direction: row`. This view is
       a column of blocks and is neither, so its heading, create form and alerts were laid out as
       columns of a page grid. Nested in the explorer's tab panel that ran past the viewport.

       Nothing replaces them: a block is what this wants, and the ghost-button border the samples
       scope under `.munin-explorer` still applies here through the explorer's own section when
       this view is mounted inside it. (Fhi.Metadata-l9l2n.39) *@
    @* Still the version, though: a host may mount this view on a page of its own, and a version
       readable on two of the three mount points is a trap for whoever checks the third.
       (Fhi.Metadata-sqbei) *@
    <div data-munin-explorer-version="@ExplorerVersion.Current">

        @* No class on the heading: the package ships no CSS, and the other components leave
           theirs bare too. A name here would be one with no rule behind it. *@
        @((MarkupString)$"<h{TitleLevel}>{T.MyListsHeading}</h{TitleLevel}>")

        @* The picker is only a choice when there is one to make. With a single list it would be a
           control whose every option is already selected. *@
        @if (Lists.Count > 1)
        {
            <label class="munin-explorer-filters__facets">
                @T.ChooseList
                <select value="@_shownList" @onchange="ChooseListAsync">
                    @foreach (var list in Lists)
                    {
                        <option value="@list.Id" selected="@(list.Id == _shownList)">@list.Name</option>
                    }
                </select>
            </label>
        }

        @* What the list on screen is, before what can be done to it. helsedata's variabellister
           page carries the same two facts on every list it draws.

           The count is the API's own TotalCount for this list, not a tally of the rows rendered —
           the endpoint is paged, so counting them would say 25 for a list of 247. There is no
           count for the lists NOT on screen: `my/lists` answers with id, name, createdAt and
           updatedAt and nothing else, so an overview showing one per list would have to invent it
           (Fhi.Metadata-2ifw8 is about the same endpoint, and is not this). *@
        @if (ListMeta is { } meta)
        {
            <p class="caption">@meta</p>
        }

        @* One row of actions, drawn the way helsedata draws its own: a single primary button for
           the one thing that makes something new, and ghost-blue for the rest. Ours wore
           button-square--ghost for all of them, which is neither variant helsedata uses here.

           Create and rename used to be standing forms — a label, a field and a button each, on
           screen whether or not the reader wanted either. They are behind their own button now,
           which is what a page with one list looked like it needed and three of. *@
        <div>
            <button class="hd-button-square button-square--primary"
                    type="button"
                    id="@CreateToggleId"
                    aria-expanded="@Expanded(_creating)"
                    @onclick="() => _creating = !_creating">@T.AddNewList</button>

            @* Both act on the list on screen, so neither is drawn when there is none. *@
            @if (_shownList is not null)
            {
                <button class="hd-button-square button-square--ghost-blue"
                        type="button"
                        id="@RenameToggleId"
                        aria-expanded="@Expanded(_renaming)"
                        @onclick="() => _renaming = !_renaming">@T.RenameList</button>

                @* Two steps because the API offers no undo, and in the page rather than a JS
                   confirm a host's Content-Security-Policy may suppress. One control saying what a
                   press does now, so neither step removes the element under the reader's focus.

                   Ghost rather than ghost-blue: it is the one control here that destroys
                   something, and drawing it in the same blue as "gi nytt navn" would say the two
                   cost the same to press. *@
                <button class="hd-button-square button-square--ghost"
                        type="button"
                        id="@DeleteButtonId"
                        aria-expanded="@Expanded(_confirmingDelete)"
                        @onclick="() => _confirmingDelete = !_confirmingDelete">
                    @(_confirmingDelete ? T.ConfirmDeleteNo : T.DeleteList)
                </button>

                @if (_confirmingDelete)
                {
                    @* The question describes the button that acts on it rather than being announced
                       on its own: a role="alert" inserted and filled in one update is announced
                       unreliably, which the alert region below is already shaped around. *@
                    <span id="@ConfirmDeleteId">@T.ConfirmDeleteList</span>
                    <button class="hd-button-square button-square--ghost"
                            type="button"
                            aria-describedby="@ConfirmDeleteId"
                            @onclick="DeleteListAsync">@T.ConfirmDeleteYes</button>
                }
            }
        </div>

        @* Revealed by the button above, and NOT closed again by a create that succeeds: the reader
           is standing on the button inside this block, and removing it would drop their focus to
           <body> — the same reason nothing here is ever `disabled`. *@
        @if (_creating)
        {
            <div>
                @* A real label with for/id, the shape the search field uses at
                   VariableSearch.razor:140 — not a placeholder. A placeholder is not an accessible
                   name: a screen reader announces an unnamed edit field, and the hint disappears
                   the moment the reader starts typing, which is the one time they most need to
                   know what the field is for. WCAG 4.1.2 and 3.3.2.

                   `form-element__label` is borrowed, not ours — Stiler's own label style, read back
                   off its compiled stylesheet and already worn by the search field and the filter
                   panel's legend (VariableSearch.razor:140, 209, 216). README's list of borrowed
                   names carries it. No new name is being invented here; a name Stiler has never
                   heard of would render as a raw browser default on a host that has only Stiler.

                   The id carries a per-mount discriminator, the shape the explorer's own ids use,
                   so two of this component on one page do not tie both labels to the first field.

                   @bind with onchange, deliberately NOT value + @oninput: this renders inside a
                   Blazor Server circuit, where oninput is one round trip per keystroke and drops
                   characters on a fast paste. The search field says the same at
                   VariableSearch.razor:146. *@
                <label class="form-element__label" for="@NewListNameId">@T.NewListName</label>
                <input type="text"
                       id="@NewListNameId"
                       @bind="_newName"
                       @bind:event="onchange" />

                @* aria-disabled rather than disabled: it becomes unavailable the moment a
                   successful create clears the name, and disabling the element that has focus
                   drops focus to <body>. CreateListAsync already refuses an empty name, so the
                   press is inert. *@
                <button class="hd-button-square button-square--ghost-blue"
                        type="button"
                        aria-disabled="@AriaDisabled(_newName.Trim().Length > 0)"
                        @onclick="CreateListAsync">@T.CreateList</button>
            </div>
        }

        @* The same shape, for the list on screen. Left open after a rename that succeeds for the
           reason the create block gives. *@
        @if (_renaming && _shownList is not null)
        {
            <div>
                <label class="form-element__label" for="@RenameListNameId">@T.RenameListName</label>
                <input type="text"
                       id="@RenameListNameId"
                       @bind="_renameName"
                       @bind:event="onchange" />
                <button class="hd-button-square button-square--ghost-blue"
                        type="button"
                        aria-disabled="@AriaDisabled(_renameName.Trim().Length > 0)"
                        @onclick="RenameListAsync">@T.SaveListName</button>
            </div>
        }

        @* Only when there is something to download. A button that exports an empty list would
           produce a file with a header row and nothing under it. *@
        @if (_page is not null && _page.TotalCount > 0)
        {
            @* A named group, not just a visual one: three controls that only make sense together,
               and a screen reader moving between them otherwise hears three unrelated widgets. *@
            <div role="group" aria-label="@T.DownloadList">
                <label>
                    <input type="checkbox"
                           checked="@_includeKodeverk"
                           @onchange="e => _includeKodeverk = e.Value is true" />
                    @T.IncludeKodeverk
                </label>

                @* Two formats, two buttons. aria-disabled while a download is in flight rather
                   than disabled, for the reason the pager gives: disabling the element that has
                   focus drops focus to <body>. Secondary actions, so ghost-blue — the same
                   variant helsedata gives "Åpne variabelliste" on their own page. *@
                <button class="hd-button-square button-square--ghost-blue"
                        type="button"
                        aria-disabled="@AriaDisabled(!_downloading)"
                        @onclick="() => DownloadAsync(ExportFormat.Xlsx)">@T.DownloadXlsx</button>

                <button class="hd-button-square button-square--ghost-blue"
                        type="button"
                        aria-disabled="@AriaDisabled(!_downloading)"
                        @onclick="() => DownloadAsync(ExportFormat.Csv)">@T.DownloadCsv</button>
            </div>
        }

        @* Always in the DOM, empty when nothing is wrong — a role="alert" inserted and filled in the
           same update is announced unreliably. Same shape as the explorer's own alert region. *@
        <div role="alert" aria-live="assertive" aria-atomic="true">
            @(_failed ? T.ListLoadError : DownloadMessage ?? ActionMessage ?? CreateMessage ?? DesiredDataMessage)
        </div>

        @* A refused annotation gets its own region, because it outlasts whatever the reader does
           next: the field stays marked invalid until that row is written again, and sharing the
           region above would let a download or a rename failure take the sentence away and leave
           the mark unexplained (WCAG 3.3.1). The refused field points here by id. *@
        <div role="alert" aria-live="assertive" aria-atomic="true" id="@DesiredDataRefusalId">
            @DesiredDataRefusalMessage
        </div>

        @* Not while the read is known to have failed. The alert above already says so, and
           "du har ingen variabellister ennå" beside it answers the same question the other way —
           the reader cannot tell whether they have none or whether we never found out. The two
           branches below are already unreachable then, because a failure leaves _page null. *@
        @if (!_failed && Lists.Count == 0)
        {
            <p>@T.NoListsYet</p>
        }
        else if (_page is not null && _page.Items.Count == 0 && !_loading)
        {
            <p>@T.EmptyList</p>
        }
        else if (_page is not null)
        {
            @* A real <table>, the shape Kelda's kilder list uses and the explorer's result list
               cannot: `munin-explorer-dataitem-main` is `display: flex` in Stiler, and flex on a
               table strips the table semantics from it (VariableSearch.razor:530). That view has
               to keep the flex row, because it is helsedata's own variable page redrawn. This one
               is not — nothing over there dresses a saved list — so it can have the element that
               brings its own structure and its own column alignment, with no rule required from
               anybody.

               The role="table" scaffolding it replaces did reach assistive technology; what it
               could not do is stop each row repeating all seven field names for a screen reader,
               because a div has no header to be associated with. Eight <th scope="col"> do that
               once, and RowCell drops the per-cell label when it is writing real cells.

               The cells wear the per-column modifier ALONE. `munin-explorer-dataitem-main__column`
               is `display: inline-flex`, which on a <td> takes the cell out of its own table; the
               modifiers are widths and the browser's table layout ignores them harmlessly. *@
            <div class="munin-explorer-list-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="@ShownListName">
                <table class="munin-explorer-data-list" aria-label="@ShownListName">
                    <thead>
                        <tr class="munin-explorer-data-list__item__row--header">
                            <th scope="col" class="munin-explorer-dataitem-header__name">@T.ColumnVariable</th>
                            <th scope="col" class="munin-explorer-dataitem-header__code">@T.FieldCode</th>
                            <th scope="col" class="munin-explorer-dataitem-header__source">@T.FieldSource</th>
                            <th scope="col" class="munin-explorer-dataitem-header__dataCollection">@T.FieldDataCollection</th>
                            <th scope="col" class="munin-explorer-dataitem-header__theme">@T.FieldVariableGroup</th>
                            <th scope="col" class="munin-explorer-dataitem-header__dataType">@T.FieldDataType</th>
                            <th scope="col" class="munin-explorer-dataitem-header__period">@T.FieldDataPeriod</th>
                            <th scope="col" class="munin-explorer-dataitem-header__desiredData">@T.FieldDesiredData</th>

                            @* A control column with no visible heading, the shape Kelda's expand
                               column uses: every button under it says "Fjern" already, and a
                               screen reader arriving in the column has nothing else to hear. *@
                            <th scope="col"><span class="screenreader-only">@T.ColumnRemove</span></th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        @foreach (var item in _page.Items)
                        {
                            <tr class="munin-explorer-data-list__item">

                                @* An entry whose variable has no row in the read model keeps its
                                   place rather than being filtered out: the API returns it on
                                   purpose so the paging totals stay honest, and a list of 247 that
                                   answered with 246 would hide that something went. It says so
                                   instead.

                                   RowName decides which of the two it is, in one place: written
                                   out twice, the cell and the remove button below — which is named
                                   from these very words — could come to say different things about
                                   the same row. CatalogueLang leaves the attribute off for the
                                   sentence, which is ours and in the reader's language, not
                                   Norwegian catalogue text.

                                   <th scope="row">, the call Kelda's own table makes: the
                                   variable's name is what the rest of the row is about, so a
                                   reader hearing one cell out of context is told which variable it
                                   belongs to. No class — `munin-explorer-dataitem-main__name` is
                                   `display: inline-flex`, which a table header cell cannot be. *@
                                <th scope="row">
                                    <span class="munin-explorer-dataitem-main__column__text"
                                          id="@RowNameId(item)"
                                          lang="@CatalogueLang(item.VariableName)">@RowName(item)</span>
                                </th>

                                @* The six columns the result list draws too, built by the cell
                                   helper both call — written out here as well, they could gain an
                                   attribute in one place and not the other without anything
                                   failing. *@
                                @Cells(item)

                                @* The one editable cell in the row, and the only column the
                                   catalogue does not own. value + @onchange rather than @bind for
                                   the reason the create field gives: inside a Blazor Server circuit
                                   oninput is a round trip per keystroke. Rendered from the draft
                                   rather than from the item, which is what keeps a text the API
                                   refused in the field instead of reverting it under the reader.

                                   The column header names the column; this span names the FIELD,
                                   which a header does not do — a control still owes an accessible
                                   name of its own (WCAG 4.1.2), and it is two elements rather than
                                   an aria-label for the reasons the remove button below gives. *@
                                <td class="munin-explorer-dataitem-main__desiredData">
                                    <span class="screenreader-only" id="@DesiredDataLabelId(item)">@T.FieldDesiredData</span>
                                    <input type="text"
                                           value="@DesiredDataOf(item)"
                                           aria-labelledby="@DesiredDataLabelledBy(item)"
                                           aria-invalid="@DesiredDataInvalid(item)"
                                           aria-describedby="@DesiredDataDescribedBy(item)"
                                           @onchange="e => SaveDesiredDataAsync(item.VariableId, e.Value?.ToString())" />
                                </td>

                                @* The visible word stays "Fjern" — the column is narrow and every
                                   button in it removes something. The accessible name says which
                                   one: without it a list of forty is forty controls that read
                                   "Fjern", and a screen reader user has no way to tell which row
                                   they are standing on. WCAG 4.1.2.

                                   Two elements rather than an aria-label, the rule the explorer's
                                   own save button follows: this button points at itself and then
                                   at the name cell, so "Fjern" stays ours and in the reader's
                                   language while the variable's name stays Munin's and marked
                                   lang="no" — one aria-label string would be a single unmarked
                                   sentence an English voice pronounces the Norwegian half of with
                                   English phonetics (WCAG 3.1.2). Itself first keeps the visible
                                   word at the front for speech input (WCAG 2.5.3).

                                   An orphaned row borrows the sentence its name cell shows
                                   instead, so the button says what it removes without reading an
                                   id out. Two orphans do then announce alike — a duplicate name
                                   on two controls is not a WCAG failure, and ten seconds of
                                   spelt-out GUID for a value the row does not show anywhere is
                                   worse than one. *@
                                <td>
                                    <button class="hd-button-square button-square--ghost-blue"
                                            type="button"
                                            id="@RemoveButtonId(item)"
                                            aria-labelledby="@RemoveLabelledBy(item)"
                                            @onclick="() => RemoveAsync(item.VariableId)">@T.RemoveFromThisList</button>
                                </td>
                            </tr>
                        }
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            @* The list is as long as the reader made it, and the endpoint is paged. A view that
               fetched page one and called it the list would show the first 25 and hide the rest
               without saying so. *@
            @if (TotalPages > 1)
            {
                <div class="munin-explorer-pagination"
                     role="navigation"
                     aria-label="@T.Pagination">
                    @* The content wrapper is where the layout lives (host.css:1609), and the
                       disabled styling is scoped to it. Without it the buttons get no flex row and
                       the unavailable state draws nothing.

                       aria-disabled, not disabled: a disabled button at the end of the list drops
                       focus to <body>, which a sighted keyboard user has no sign of. The clamp in
                       GoToPageAsync is what makes the press inert. Same rule as the result list. *@
                    <div class="munin-explorer-pagination-content">
                        <button class="hd-button-square button-square--ghost"
                                type="button"
                                aria-label="@T.PreviousLabel"
                                aria-disabled="@AriaDisabled(_pageNumber > 1)"
                                @onclick="() => GoToPageAsync(_pageNumber - 1)">@T.Previous</button>

                        @* The same numbered run the result list draws, from the same renderer.
                           This pager wears the same two class names, so it had the same unstyled
                           shape and is the same defect twice (Fhi.Metadata-ejcbi). *@
                        @PageNumbers.Write(this, _pageNumber, TotalPages, GoToPageAsync, T.GoToPageLabel, T.CurrentPageLabel)

                        <button class="hd-button-square button-square--ghost"
                                type="button"
                                aria-label="@T.NextLabel"
                                aria-disabled="@AriaDisabled(_pageNumber < TotalPages)"
                                @onclick="() => GoToPageAsync(_pageNumber + 1)">@T.Next</button>
                    </div>
                </div>
            }
        }
    </div>
}
